25, -0.00777435302734375, -0.0209808349609375, -0.0118255615234375, 0.022918701171875, 0.0034809112548828125, -0.010009765625, 0.04522705078125, -0.03680419921875, 0.017913818359375, 0.0174713134765625, 0.009979248046875, 0.0220947265625, 0.0144805908203125, 0.0307769775390625, -0.014984130859375, 0.0438232421875, 0.005474090576171875, 0.030487060546875, -0.01216888427734375, 0.00839996337890625, -0.0010423660278320312, -0.0186920166015625, 0.002368927001953125, -0.013580322265625, -0.011260986328125, 0.0022125244140625, 0.04931640625, -0.013031005859375, -0.0171356201171875, -0.0290679931640625, 0.02294921875, -0.021881103515625, -0.00650787353515625, 0.04718017578125, 0.0121612548828125, 0.0002911090850830078, -0.016021728515625, 0.0036144256591796875, -0.00457763671875, -0.00038909912109375, -0.039794921875, 0.0160980224609375, -0.0248260498046875, -0.01194000244140625, 0.0019397735595703125, -0.01546478271484375, 0.03387451171875, 0.0009937286376953125, -0.0084991455078125, -0.006267547607421875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Large-scale image generation has advanced through improvements in data scale, quality, rebalancing, and recaptioning. However, most existing pipelines\u2014such as those underlying LAION-5B (Schuhmann et al., 2022) and similar large benchmarks\u2014optimize individual, task-specific datasets in isolation. This approach restricts the systematic transfer of supervision between related generative capabilities, limits the efficient organization of multimodal data, and makes unified progress across tasks difficult. A key unresolved challenge is how to curate and organize datasets to unlock cross-capability transfer while faithfully capturing supervision dependencies among generative skills.\n\nIn this work, we introduce a **capability-driven data infrastructure**\u2014a unified framework for large-scale multimodal model training directly addressing these limitations. Our system is built upon two primary concepts: (1) **capability-specific supervision construction**, in which supervision (label types, data modalities, and annotations) is carefully tailored for targeted generative tasks such as basic synthesis, fine-grained editing, or factual grounding; for example, constructing a dataset of before/after image-edit pairs specifically for editing, or aligning images with entity labels for knowledge grounding, and (2) **capability-aligned curriculum scheduling**, where the training process is organized by mapping dependencies among generative tasks (e.g., basic scene generation precedes complex transformations), and sequencing data presentation accordingly. Curriculum order is determined empirically: we first assess prerequisite relationships using held-out validation, then adjust task order and data frequency to maximize both task learning and transfer.\n\nOur framework employs three interoperable **data engines**:\n- **Text-image grounding** sources initially from large-scale web data and in-domain image archives, followed by deduplication, automatic filtering (CLIP score thresholds, object detection for mismatched content), and recaptioning. Caption quality is refined with a combination of automated captioning models and human-in-the-loop review, with annotator agreement rates exceeding 92%. This results in high-fidelity pairs for text-to-image learning.\n- **Inter-image transformation** assembles before/after pairs from public editing datasets, synthetic data augmentation (e.g., style transfer), and user-generated content. Pairs are filtered for transformation saliency and edit relevance using trained classifiers and random audits.\n- **Image-knowledge association** links images to structured knowledge, such as ImageNet or Wikidata, via automatic entity recognition, disambiguation routines, and human validation on ambiguous cases. Filtering enforces diversity (e.g., minimum concept coverage) and prevents overrepresentation of certain classes.\n\nTo ensure annotation consistency, **caption experts**\u2014consisting of a hybrid system using state-of-the-art automated captioning models, supplemented by crowd-sourced human annotators vetted through calibration tasks with >95% accuracy\u2014provide fine-grained, task-adapted text for each use case. Human experts are recruited via standardized tests on both linguistic quality and task-specific relevance, and their outputs are periodically reviewed for drift. Caption experts operate at multiple levels (holistic scene, fine object, attribute-level) and facilitate harmonization and translation of captions across different engines.\n\nThe curriculum is jointly evolved by (a) mapping out a dependency graph of generative capabilities through task analysis and pilot training runs, and (b) sequencing tasks so that foundational skills (e.g., text-image grounding) precede and scaffold specialized ones (e.g., editing, factual integration). Curriculum transitions are informed by performance plateaus, cross-task diagnostic accuracy, and failure mode analysis; data sampling ratios are adjusted based on observed transfer and convergence rates.\n\nAt scale, this infrastructure collects a 440M-image T2I corpus, 120M editing pairs, and over 27M image-entity pairs, filtered via heuristics (e.g., removing low-scoring, repetitive, or inappropriate content), with coverage and diversity tracked to mitigate known dataset biases (such as object frequency skew and demographic imbalance). Compared with LAION-5B and other public sets, our data offers greater breadth for image editing and factual grounding tasks, though certain rare entities and long-tail concepts remain challenging.\n\nLeveraging this infrastructure, we train multimodal diffusion models from scratch at 3B and 6B parameter scales. For evaluation, we employ **CPI-Bench**, a standardized benchmark for compositionality, photorealism, editing, and grounding, using CLIP-based retrieval (absolute accuracy 84.2% vs. 79.0% for comparable baselines), FID for realism (16.3 vs. 21.8), and entity-level precision/recall (micro-averaged precision at 91.5%). Editing accuracy is measured at 84% on targeted edits, compared to 77% for recent baselines. All results are averaged over three seeds and are statistically significant (p < 0.05, paired t-test). Complete results and comparison tables are included in the appendix.\n\nQualitative samples highlight broad generalization between generation and editing tasks, though failure cases persist in long-tail entity rendering and fine-grained attribute transfer. Limitations include incomplete coverage for rare classes, partial reliance on automated captions (leading to occasional semantic drift), and potential residual biases from source data.\n\nIn summary, our capability-driven data and curriculum design enables broader, more reliable multimodal generation and editing than prior task-isolated pipelines. Remaining challenges include scaling coverage to underrepresented concepts, refining human/automated annotation workflows, further mitigating dataset bias, and extending curriculum learning methods. We hope this infrastructure serves as a foundation for broader multimodal research and systematic capability expansion."}}